<p>After many failed attempts, I am finally comfortable doing all of my computing using Linux. In case you haven't heard, Linux is good now! And yet, I'm back on my Mac...for now.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>The short version? It's the software. Linux is loaded with pretty good apps, many of them free. Thing is, nearly all of them also run on macOS. This means that on a Mac, I can use all the great macOS software, and most of the great apps I use on Linux..</p>
<p>I've spent the past year recreating the dozens of little workflows on Linux that I'd built for myself on the Mac. Some of them were easily portable, but many weren't. Some had no real replacements, so I had to give them up.</p>
<p>The things I like about Linux are abstract. The &quot;I'm in complete control of my computing environment&quot; kind of things. I miss that feeling on the Mac, especially when I can't launch an app that I downloaded or when I've had to dismiss the two thousandth permissions dialog that day. Also, I love the &quot;This twelve-year-old ThinkPad still works great running Linux!&quot;</p>
<p>What I don't miss are all the Linux distros. I mean, what an embarrassment of riches, right? Except my brain never stops thinking &quot;What if I switched to...?&quot; every other day. Half the time I actually have switched, with disastrous effects on my time. I don't enjoy the community bickering either. &quot;Systemd sucks!&quot; and so on. And there's still too much &quot;Um, actually...&quot; and &quot;Why don't you just...?&quot;</p>
<p>Overall, my year-long adventure with Linux was fun and successful. But I don't want to give up some of the Mac software I've used for years...sometimes <em>many</em> years.</p>
<p>I don't want to lose <a href="https://eastgate.com/Tinderbox">Tinderbox</a> or <a href="https://www.devontechnologies.com/apps/devonthink">DEVONthink</a> or <a href="https://www.zengobi.com/curio/">Curio</a> or <a href="https://captureone.com/">Capture One</a> or <a href="https://www.silverfast.com/">SilverFast</a> or <a href="https://www.omnigroup.com/omnifocus/">OmniFocus</a> or any number of nifty little utilities.</p>
<p>What might be the biggest draw of macOS for me is that I automatically get Emacs key bindings everywhere. I hadn't realized how much I relied on them until they were gone.</p>
<p>I'm sure I'll revisit this again at some point. I still have a couple of nice laptops running Linux, and I'll certainly pop them open occasionally, but for now, I'm going to live on my Mac.</p>
<p>I know some of you have been rooting for me to fully adopt Linux. Please don't be too disappointed. Everything with me is temporary :). At least I know I can switch completely to Linux if I want to. I'm just not ready.</p>
